# libopenwch formatting (clang-format is the authority on style)
#
# clang-format is the authority on formatting.  The tree is kept clean, so
# `clang-format --dry-run -Werror` over any .c/.h file should report nothing.
#
# To format as you go, copy libopenwch's .git/hooks/pre-commit into
# .git/hooks/ here: it formats the files you are about to commit, and does
# nothing at all when clang-format is not installed.  Nothing in CI formats or
# checks formatting, on purpose -- a build should not depend on which optional
# tools the machine happens to have.
#
# What that produces for function definitions:
#
#     int foo(int a) {
#     }
#
# and, when a signature does not fit in 80 columns, a break after the
# declaration specifiers with the parameters aligned under the open paren:
#
#     __attribute__((weak)) void
#     bar(int aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa,
#         int bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb) {
#     	foo(a);
#     }
#
# Two things clang-format cannot express, which were therefore given up when
# it became the formatter:
#
#   * a closing ") {" on a line of its own -- no such option exists, so
#     parameters align under the open paren and ')' stays on the last one;
#   * GNU __attribute__((...)) on a line of its own -- BreakAfterAttributes
#     below applies to C++ [[...]] attributes and leaves GNU ones attached.
#
# BreakAfterAttributes needs clang-format 17 or newer; older releases ignore it.
BasedOnStyle: LLVM
IndentWidth: 8
TabWidth: 8
UseTab: Always
ColumnLimit: 80
BreakBeforeBraces: Attach
BreakAfterAttributes: Always
AllowShortIfStatementsOnASingleLine: false
AllowShortLoopsOnASingleLine: true
AllowShortFunctionsOnASingleLine: false
BinPackParameters: false
AllowAllParametersOfDeclarationOnNextLine: false
IndentCaseLabels: false
PointerAlignment: Right
AlignAfterOpenBracket: Align
SpaceBeforeParens: ControlStatements
SortIncludes: false
ReflowComments: false
